    },
    {
      h2: 'How Refresher times a round',
      paras: [
        "The defaults are 30 power breaths at 3 seconds each, 1.5 in and 1.5 out, then a retention of 60 seconds in round one and 90 in rounds two and three, and a 15-second recovery hold. Three rounds come to just over nine minutes.",
        "The retention is a countdown, and you're in charge of it. When the urge to breathe comes early, tap Skip to Recovery and the app moves straight to the recovery breath. When you've got more in you, tap +10 Seconds, as many times as you like. Both work for everyone, free or not. The app saves the hold you actually did.",
        "A voice runs the whole session. It calls in and out on every breath, then ten more, five more and the last one, on the longer holds tells you when you're halfway, then when 30 seconds are left, counts the final five, and says let go at the end of the recovery. On iPhone each power breath also lands as a haptic tap, heavier on the inhale, building over the last five.",
      ],
      aside: [
        "I made every number in a Wim Hof session adjustable. You can set 10 to 60 power breaths, a breath speed from 2.6 to 4 seconds, 1 to 10 rounds, a recovery hold of 10 to 20 seconds, and a retention for each round on its own, from 30 seconds to 5 minutes in 15-second steps.",
        "I also made the voice the only session mode for this one. Every other paced technique in the app can run to music. Wim Hof can't: a power breath lasts a second and a half, and nobody can count thirty of them against a song. Silence doesn't work either. It leaves you staring at a circle, trying to remember whether that was breath 22 or 23.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'After the last recovery breath',
      paras: [
        "The completion screen shows your longest hold of the session, and tells you when it beats your record. Each session in your history lists the hold you actually did in every round. On Apple Watch the session runs the default holds with heart rate. Custom holds and your hold history live on iPhone.",
        "With an Apple Watch and heart rate tracking switched on in Settings, the session opens and closes with a heart rate reading and the difference lands on the completion screen. If the Watch misses either end, the row is left out. With Premium, the session's heart rate chart is split into rounds, retentions and recoveries, so you can see where each hold falls on the line.",
        "Changing the numbers is free for your first five sessions of a minute or longer, then it's part of Premium, and your saved settings are kept. The default protocol stays free. Adding a round when you reach the last one is Premium.",
